WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.430
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:03.402
ERIC CHIANG: Hi.

00:00:03.402 --> 00:00:04.510
My name's Eric Chiang.

00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:05.926
I'm a security and
privacy product

00:00:05.926 --> 00:00:08.330
manager at Google
focused on data deletion.

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:12.360
Generally speaking, Google
removes data in three stages.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:14.630
Stage 1 is the deletion request.

00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:17.540
This is when the user
issues a deletion request,

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:20.540
either via the Cloud
Console or via the API,

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:23.150
and a piece of data is
flagged for deletion.

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:26.180
Stage 2 is the data
removal and recovery.

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:28.130
Once a deletion request
is made, the data

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:30.110
is made inaccessible
to read requests

00:00:30.110 --> 00:00:32.509
and it is hidden from the
product user interface.

00:00:32.509 --> 00:00:35.420
This removal step confirms
the user's deletion request.

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:37.680
Some deletion requests
allow for recovery,

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:40.940
such as those for accounts,
projects, or a query

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:42.650
on BigQuery.

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:44.697
Stage 3 is the deletion.

00:00:44.697 --> 00:00:46.280
If the data marked
for deletion is not

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:48.350
recovered within
the stage 2 window,

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:50.870
it is deleted in the
underlying layer of products,

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:53.330
such as Cloud Storage
or Cloud Spanner.

00:00:53.330 --> 00:00:55.640
Each GCP service from
which data gets deleted

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:59.210
has its own detailed process
to ensure complete deletion.

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:01.070
This might involve
repeated passes

00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:05.129
through the system to confirm
all data has been deleted.

00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:08.160
After the recovery period,
GCP will broadcast a deletion

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.170
request with a unique
account or project

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:12.400
identifier to all subservices.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:14.310
This will begin the
product-level deletion,

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:17.680
during which GCP waits for an
acknowledgment signal from all

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:19.800
the products to
complete deletion.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:22.080
Most of the products within
GCP performs deletion

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:23.100
by overwriting.

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:25.960
This is done by removing the
file pointer to the data.

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:28.740
And once the data is marked as
deleted, the space it occupied

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:31.170
is marked as empty and
eventually overwritten

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:32.520
by new data.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:35.160
Google Cloud Storage
goes one step further

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.530
by activating cryptographic
erasure, which

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:40.080
deletes the key that is
used to decrypt the storage

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:42.840
buckets before deletion
of the buckets' content

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:44.430
by overwriting it.

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:46.020
Last but not least,
let's not forget

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:48.300
de-commissioning of hardware.

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:50.910
Google meticulously tracks
the location and status

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:52.980
of all equipment
within our data centers

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:56.190
through acquisition,
installation, retirement,

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:59.400
and destruction, via
barcodes and asset tags.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:02.250
If a component fails to pass a
performance test at any point

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:05.100
during its lifecycle, it is
removed from the inventory

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:06.267
and retired.

00:02:06.267 --> 00:02:08.100
When it is retired, an
authorized individual

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:10.350
will verify that
the disk is erased

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:12.420
by overwriting the
drive with zeros

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:14.340
and performing a
multi-step verification

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:17.790
process to ensure that the
drive contains no data.

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:20.010
If the drive cannot be
erased for any reason,

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:22.950
it is stored securely until it
can be physically destroyed.

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:24.600
Depending on
available equipment,

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:26.760
we either crush and
deform the drive

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:28.890
or shred the drive
into small pieces.

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:32.130
In either case, the disk is
recycled at a secure facility,

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:34.440
ensuring that no one
will be able to read data

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:36.180
on retired Google disks.

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:38.790
To learn more about deletion on
Google Cloud Platform, please

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:40.717
visit our website and
read our white paper.

00:02:40.717 --> 00:02:41.550
Thanks for watching.

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:43.700
[MUSIC PLAYING]

